<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>Quiz 6 - MTH 305 - Regan</title>

    <p>
        This quiz covers single equation ODE models, their fixed point(s), and
        stability and a retake on optimization of mathematical models.
        Read all <term>DIRECTIONS</term> carefully.
        Good luck!
    </p>

    <exercise workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the fixed point(s) for the following differential equation
                and determine the corresponding stability.
                <md>
                    \frac{dx}{dt}= x - ax^{2}
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Here, <m>a \gt 0</m> is a parameter.
                Show all work and state in a sentence whether you think the
                stability of the fixed point(s) depends on the value of the
                parameter <m>a</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider the following graph.
            </p>

            <image source="images/06quiz-1.png" width="40%">
                <shortdescription>
                    Graph of multiple solution trajectories for the differential
                    equation dy/dt = sin(y) given different initial conditions.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>

            <p>
                Identify the fixed points and whether they are stable or
                unstable.
                Explain your reasoning.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <p>
        <term>Retake of Optimization: </term>
    </p>

    <exercise workspace="5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                We want to construct a cylindrical can with a bottom, but no top
                that will have a volume of <m>30 \,\, cm^3</m>.
                Determine the dimensions of the can that will minimize the
                amount of material needed to construct the can.
            </p>

            <p>
                Remember the volume of a cylinder is given by
                <m>{V = \pi r^2h.}</m> Additionally, the surface area of a
                cylinder with a top and a bottom is given by
                <m>{A = 2\pi rh + 2\pi r^2.}</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>